# Copy this file to `.env` before running `docker compose up` (see README
# "Quickstart"). Every variable below is read by the app via
# pydantic-settings, unprefixed (backend/app/config.py) -- the sole
# exception is IMAGE_TAG in the next section, which Compose itself
# consumes. Interval/size variables carry no unit suffix -- the unit is
# noted in each comment.

# --- Published image selection (compose.prod.yaml) -------------------------
# Chooses WHICH published image the production overlay runs:
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml up -d
# Ignored entirely without `-f compose.prod.yaml` (the default compose.yaml
# builds from source and tags the result `tdmm:local`).
#
# Unlike everything else here this is NOT an app setting -- it is expanded
# by Compose into compose.prod.yaml's `image:` line. Compose auto-loads the
# root `.env` for that interpolation, which is why setting it here works;
# it is NOT delivered through `env_file`, so the containers never see it. A
# shell override (`IMAGE_TAG=v0.1.0 docker compose ... up -d`) wins over
# this file.
#
# Defaults to `latest` (newest stable release, and it MOVES). Pin an exact
# release for anything you care about, so an upgrade is something you
# choose rather than something that happens to you:
#IMAGE_TAG=v0.1.0
#IMAGE_TAG=latest

# --- Container privilege drop (Task 9) -------------------------------------
# Unset by default: the api/worker containers run as root (unchanged M1
# choice). Set BOTH to your host user's `id -u`/`id -g` to have the
# entrypoint drop to that uid/gid instead, so files written under the
# bind-mounted ./library aren't root-owned on the host.
#PUID=1000
#PGID=1000

# --- Database -----------------------------------------------------------
# Points at the `db` compose service. The embedded user/password/dbname
# MUST match POSTGRES_USER / POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB below.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://tdmm:tdmm@db:5432/tdmm
# Compose interpolation (never reaches the app): credentials the `db` service
# is created with, and the ones the host-networked virtual-printer builds its
# own DATABASE_URL from. Defaults are tdmm/tdmm/tdmm; if you change them,
# change DATABASE_URL above to match (and do so BEFORE the first `up` -- an
# existing pgdata volume keeps its original credentials).
#POSTGRES_USER=tdmm
#POSTGRES_PASSWORD=tdmm
#POSTGRES_DB=tdmm

# --- Reverse proxy (bootstrap; read by docker/entrypoint.sh) ---------------
# uvicorn runs with --proxy-headers and trusts X-Forwarded-* only from these
# addresses. Default 127.0.0.1 (trust nobody on the docker network). When a
# reverse proxy on the docker network terminates TLS, set this to the proxy's
# IP/CIDR (or `*` if the api is reachable only through it) so the request
# scheme is seen as https. Alternative: set the Public URL to https in
# Settings -> Server (the cookie Secure flag follows it). The same setting makes
# the login throttle see real client IPs instead of the proxy's.
#FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS=127.0.0.1

# --- Redis (Celery broker/results + SSE pub/sub) -------------------------
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0

# --- Storage --------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths as seen INSIDE the api/worker containers (compose mounts these).
LIBRARY_ROOT=/library
DATA_DIR=/data

# Storage backends (M3): the active backend -- local (default), SMB, or S3
# -- is NOT configured here. It's selected and configured from the running
# app's Settings > Storage UI (connection-tested before it takes effect,
# with a local->X migration helper to copy existing content over). There is
# always exactly one active backend, stored in the `settings` table.
#
# SMB: address the NAS by IP or a real, resolvable DNS name (never a bare
# mDNS/.local/NetBIOS name -- the container's resolver won't find it). For
# a LAN-only DNS name, add it to the `api`/`worker-io`/`worker-cpu` services
# in compose.yaml, e.g.:
#   extra_hosts:
#     - "nas.lan:192.168.1.50"
#
# --- Runtime feature settings (Settings UI, DB-backed) ---------------------
# The printer toggle and the scan / collection-sync / watched-folder poll
# schedules used to be env vars set here. They now live in the running app
# under Settings (General -> Automation & scheduling; Printer -> Printer
# integration), are stored in the database, and take effect within ~15s
# with no restart of anything required.
#
# On the very first boot only (no settings row exists yet), the app seeds
# those settings from the env names below if present in the environment,
# then ignores them on every boot after that (a startup warning names any
# of these still set): PRINTER_ENABLED, SCAN_INTERVAL,
# COLLECTION_SYNC_INTERVAL, WATCH_INTERVAL, WATCH_STABLE. There's no reason
# to set them for a fresh install -- configure everything in Settings once
# the app is up instead.

# --- Printer integration (M4, Bambu LAN) -----------------------------------
# OFF by default: the whole app is fully functional without it (the printers
# API 503s, the Printer nav hides). Toggle it live, no restart, from
# Settings -> Printer in the running app -- the printerd service always
# runs and idles harmlessly with the toggle off. Requires the printer in
# LAN-only + Developer Mode (see README "Printer integration").
#
# Fernet key for the printer access code at rest. Leave unset to let the app
# generate + persist one at ${DATA_DIR}/secrets/printer.key (0600),
# shared across api/worker/printerd via the tdmm_data volume. Set it here
# instead (a urlsafe-base64 32-byte key) to supply your own.
#PRINTER_KEY=

# --- Watched-folder auto-import (Round 8 Task 5) ---------------------------
# Periodically imports Bambu Studio (or any slicer's) exported files dropped
# into a watched folder, resolving each to a model the same way
# `POST /api/slicer/intake` does (see scripts/bambu_postprocess.py for the
# post-processing-script alternative, which doesn't need this at all).
# `WATCH_DIR` itself is NOT set here -- compose.yaml already sets
# it to `/watch` inside the `worker-io` container; leave it alone. The poll
# interval and the mtime-stability window are configured from Settings ->
# General -> Automation & scheduling in the running app, not here (see the
# "Runtime feature settings" block above).
#
# Host directory bind-mounted at `/watch` inside `worker-io` (compose.yaml) --
# point your slicer's export/sync target at this path. Must be writable by
# PUID/PGID: imported/unsupported files are moved into `.imported/`/
# `.failed/` subdirectories under it in place.
#WATCH_HOST_DIR=./slicer-watch

# --- SPA static files -----------------------------------------------------
# Where the built frontend lives inside the image. The Dockerfile already
# sets this by default (ENV STATIC_DIR=/app/static); it's spelled out
# here for documentation. Leave unset for local `uv run uvicorn`
# development -- the Vite dev server serves the frontend there instead.
STATIC_DIR=/app/static

# --- Auth -----------------------------------------------------------------
# FIRST-BOOT SEED ONLY: ADMIN_USERNAME/ADMIN_PASSWORD create the admin
# account on the very first boot (no user row yet) and are ignored after
# that -- change the password afterwards from Settings -> General ->
# Password, not by editing .env.
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# Leave ADMIN_PASSWORD unset (commented out) on first run: the api
# container generates a random password and prints it ONCE to its logs
# (`docker compose logs api`) -- copy it down immediately, it's not
# recoverable after that. Set it explicitly here instead to pin a known
# password (e.g. for scripting -- scripts/e2e.sh requires this).
#ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Printer/process/filament profiles can be imported from a Bambu Studio
# profile bundle (a zip or folder with a BBL.json manifest -- the same
# tree Bambu Studio exports/keeps under its own config directory) via
# `/inventory/profiles`'s Import dialog, or pulled straight from the
# running sidecar (no env var needed for either path).

# QUEUES / CONCURRENCY / MAX_TASKS_PER_CHILD / MAX_MEMORY_PER_CHILD
# (the last one in KiB, matching Celery's --max-memory-per-child) are
# compose-managed (set directly in compose.yaml's per-service
# `environment:`, one set for `worker-io`, a different set for
# `worker-cpu`) -- do not set them here, .env is shared by every service.

# --- Sidecars (always on; compose.slicer.yaml / compose.obico.yaml) -------------
# The slicer, obico-ml and virtual-printer containers always start -- there
# are no compose profiles. Whether the app USES them (and their URLs) is set in
# Settings -> Server, not here. They cost RAM (slicer: 4g limit) and disk (the
# obico-ml image is large); `docker compose stop slicer obico-ml` reclaims them.
#
# The remaining knobs are Compose interpolation only (never reach the app):
# Slicer image. Default `tdmm-slicer:local` is built from ./slicer. With
# compose.prod.yaml the published ghcr.io/metril/3d-model-manager-slicer:${IMAGE_TAG}
# is used instead. Override to pin another tag:
#SLICER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/metril/3d-model-manager-slicer:edge
# Concurrent slices the sidecar accepts (extra requests get 429 and the
# queue retries later), the per-slice wall-clock limit in seconds, memory cap
# and /tmp size.
#SLICER_MAX_JOBS=1
#SLICER_JOB_TIMEOUT_S=600
#SLICER_MEM_LIMIT=4g
#SLICER_TMP_SIZE=1g
# Obico ML image for dev/local builds (compose.prod.yaml uses the published
# ghcr.io/metril/3d-model-manager-obico-ml:${IMAGE_TAG} instead).
#OBICO_ML_IMAGE=tdmm-obico-ml:local

# Printer/process/filament profiles can be imported from a Bambu Studio
# profile bundle (a zip or folder with a BBL.json manifest) via
# `/inventory/profiles`'s Import dialog, or pulled straight from the running
# sidecar (no env var needed for either path).

# QUEUES / CONCURRENCY / MAX_TASKS_PER_CHILD / MAX_MEMORY_PER_CHILD
# (the last one in KiB, matching Celery's --max-memory-per-child) are
# compose-managed (set directly in compose.yaml's per-service
# `environment:`, one set for `worker-io`, a different set for
# `worker-cpu`) -- do not set them here, .env is shared by every service.

# --- Virtual printer host ports (Compose interpolation) -------------------
# The virtual printer is host-networked (SSDP discovery), so it reaches db and
# redis through loopback-only published ports (127.0.0.1) instead of the
# `db`/`redis` service names. Change these only on a port clash.
#TDMM_DB_HOST_PORT=15432
#TDMM_REDIS_HOST_PORT=16379

# --- API host port (Compose interpolation) ---------------------------------
# Host port the web UI/API is published on (container port stays 8080).
# Change only on a port clash.
#TDMM_API_HOST_PORT=8080

# =============================================================================
# FIRST-BOOT SEED ONLY -- ignored afterwards; edit Settings -> Server instead.
# =============================================================================
# Each variable below seeds the matching Settings -> Server field exactly once,
# on the very first boot (no `server` settings row yet). After that the
# database wins: changing or leaving these here does nothing, and a startup
# warning names any still set. None are needed for a fresh install -- just
# configure Settings -> Server once the app is up.
#
#TDMM_PUBLIC_URL=https://models.example.com   # Public URL (also the OIDC callback origin)
#MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=4294967296                  # Max upload size, bytes
#MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES=2147483648                # Max importer download size, bytes
#SSE_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=15                    # seconds
#TDMM_SLICER_URL=http://slicer:3001           # Slicer URL (setting it also enables the slicer)
#TDMM_SLICE_TIMEOUT_S=600                     # seconds
#TDMM_OBICO_URL=http://obico-ml:3333          # Obico ML URL
#TDMM_OBICO_CALLBACK=http://api:8000          # Obico callback URL
#TDMM_VP_BIND=0.0.0.0                         # Virtual printer bind address
#TDMM_VP_PORT_SSDP=2021
#TDMM_VP_PORT_MQTT=8883
#TDMM_VP_PORT_FTP=990
#TDMM_VP_PASV_MIN=50000
#TDMM_VP_PASV_MAX=50009
#TDMM_VP_PASV_ADDRESS=
# Outgoing mail (2FA email codes, notifications). Seeded once; edit in Settings -> Server.
#TDMM_SMTP_HOST=
#TDMM_SMTP_PORT=587
#TDMM_SMTP_SECURITY=starttls   # none | starttls | tls
#TDMM_SMTP_USERNAME=
#TDMM_SMTP_PASSWORD=
#TDMM_SMTP_FROM=
# Removed (no replacement env var): COOKIE_SECURE (derived from the Public URL /
# request scheme) and TDMM_VP_ENABLED (the virtual printer is always available).
